{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790985524,"nonce":"2UgVHd6ntezMMomb/+Yhq37F7vc=","title":"Offscreen captures are lighter than the window: gamma encoded twice","message":"`render_to_rgba8` and every other headless render came back visibly\nlighter than the same UI on screen.\n\nBlinc's shaders bake the gamma transfer at the source: text glyphs, SDF\nfills and layer composites all emit sRGB-encoded values. `renderer.rs`\ntherefore goes out of its way to pick a NON-sRGB surface for a window,\nand says why: an sRGB target treats those already-encoded values as\nlinear and applies the curve again, so every colour washes out. Linux\nusers reported exactly that before the preference was made\nunconditional.\n\nThe headless path defaulted to `Bgra8UnormSrgb`, which is that bug.\nMeasured: Hybrid's primary `#2A63E9` read back as (113, 167, 245), and\nthe page `#F6F8FC` as (251, 252, 254). A second sRGB encode of the\nfirst, to the digit.\n\nTwo reasons it went unnoticed:\n\n- `render_to_rgba8_returns_packed_pixels` renders pure red, and 255 is a\n  fixed point of the sRGB curve, so it cannot see the difference.\n- `blinc_test_suite`'s harness already overrode the format to\n  `Rgba8Unorm`, so the one in-repo consumer that cares about exact\n  pixels had opted out.\n\nIt matters for anything comparing a capture against a window:\nscreenshots, visual-regression baselines, and cross-renderer\ncomparison.\n\nFound by the ashui session while comparing its own offscreen render of a\nnine-shape corner scene against Blinc's GPU path.","files":null}]}